WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:08.000
ChicGrasp: a custom dual-jaw gripper and a diffusion policy.
Published study: 113 successful trials out of 140.

00:08.000 --> 00:16.000
Local CAD assembly. Two jaw groups operate independently.
Only the presentation camera moves around the model.

00:16.000 --> 00:24.000
The study reports 100 demonstrations.
Three camera views and robot and jaw state condition the policy.

00:24.000 --> 00:30.000
Actual offline checkpoint replay at a recorded observation from 5 seconds.
Gaussian noise is refined into robot and jaw targets.

00:30.000 --> 00:36.000
The next replay uses an observation from 12 seconds.
Each point represents a future Cartesian target.

00:36.000 --> 00:42.000
At 20 seconds, the predicted jaw sequence includes a right-jaw closure.
These are newly sampled offline actions, not executed commands.

00:42.000 --> 00:52.000
Recorded commands, shown at half speed.
Right jaw closes at 20.6 seconds; left jaw closes at 21.1 seconds.

00:52.000 --> 00:59.000
Published diffusion-policy results: 84 of 100 seen trials,
29 of 40 unseen trials, and 113 of 140 overall.

00:59.000 --> 01:05.000
Both baselines report zero successful cycles in this study.
Rehanging is scripted; the reported total cycle is about 38 seconds.

01:05.000 --> 01:15.000
Separate ongoing Isaac Lab work: articulated anatomy and shackle contact.
This cinematic shows a held pose; a released two-hock hang is unverified.

01:15.000 --> 01:24.000
Amirreza Davar. Mechanical design, data collection, policy training,
real-robot integration, and experimental evaluation.
